Rāma-soothed Sītā reproaches her broad-chested husband --- Sītā asks if Janaka took woman in man's form for son-in-law --- Sītā likens herself to Sāvitrī following Satyavant at her own will --- Sītā insists the forest heaven lies only beside Rāma --- Sītā counts thorns as cotton and dust as sandal beside him --- Sītā counts forest fare as ambrosia, heaven with Rāma, hell without --- Sītā vows poison now rather than fall to foes, cannot bear fourteen years --- Sītā clings weeping shedding crystal tears like lotus water --- Rāma embraces grieving Sītā and grants forest leave --- Rāma bids her give jewels and food to brāhmaṇas and hasten
